  glossary: {
    'neuron': 'A nerve cell. Neurons carry electrical signals and pass messages to each other at synapses. The adult human brain has roughly 86 billion of them.',
    'synapse': 'The tiny junction where one neuron passes a chemical message to the next. Memory depends on synapses forming and strengthening; in Alzheimer\'s they are lost early.',
    'amyloid-beta': 'A short protein fragment (usually 40 or 42 building blocks long) cut from a larger protein called APP. It sticks to itself, forming clumps that range from pairs to huge plaques.',
    'APP': 'Amyloid precursor protein: a normal protein that sits in the membrane of neurons. Two enzymes snip it, releasing the amyloid-beta fragment.',
    'secretase': 'An enzyme that cuts APP. Beta-secretase makes the first cut and gamma-secretase the second, releasing amyloid-beta.',
    'oligomer': 'A small clump of a few amyloid-beta molecules. Soluble, mobile, and thought by many researchers to be among the most toxic forms.',
    'protofibril': 'A larger soluble clump of amyloid-beta, a short chain on its way to becoming a fibril. Lecanemab binds protofibrils about ten times more tightly than finished fibrils.',
    'fibril': 'A long, insoluble, highly ordered strand of amyloid-beta. Fibrils pile up into plaques.',
    'plaque': 'A deposit of amyloid-beta fibrils between neurons, visible under a microscope or on an amyloid PET scan. One of the two hallmarks of Alzheimer\'s.',
    'tau tangle': 'A twisted bundle of tau protein inside a neuron. Tangles spread through the brain in a predictable pattern and track symptoms closely.',
    'hippocampus': 'A seahorse-shaped structure deep in the temporal lobe that is essential for forming new memories. One of the first areas damaged in Alzheimer\'s.',
    'microglia': 'The brain\'s resident immune cells. They patrol, engulf debris and, when an antibody tags amyloid, can swallow it.',
    'mild cognitive impairment': 'Memory or thinking problems that are noticeable and measurable but do not yet stop someone living independently. When caused by Alzheimer\'s, it is the earliest symptomatic stage.',
    'dementia': 'A decline in memory and thinking severe enough to interfere with daily life. Alzheimer\'s is the most common cause.',
    'preclinical Alzheimer\'s': 'The stage when amyloid (and sometimes tau) is building up in the brain but the person has no symptoms. It can last 15 years or more.',
    'CDR-SB': 'Clinical Dementia Rating, Sum of Boxes. A clinician scores six areas of life (memory, orientation, judgment, community affairs, home and hobbies, personal care) from 0 to 3 and adds them: 0 is normal, 18 is severe dementia. Higher is worse.',
    'ADAS-Cog14': 'Alzheimer\'s Disease Assessment Scale, cognitive subscale (14-item version): a battery of memory, language and attention tasks scored 0 to 90. Higher is worse.',
    'MMSE': 'Mini-Mental State Examination: a quick 30-point bedside test of orientation, memory and attention. Lower is worse. Widely used to screen, too coarse to detect small drug effects.',
    'ADCS-MCI-ADL': 'A caregiver questionnaire about everyday activities (managing money, using appliances, finding belongings), designed for early disease. Scored 0 to 53; lower is worse.',
    'ADCOMS': 'Alzheimer\'s Disease Composite Score: a weighted mix of items from the CDR, ADAS-Cog and MMSE that Eisai built to be sensitive to early decline. Used as the primary endpoint of lecanemab\'s phase 2.',
    'iADRS': 'Integrated Alzheimer\'s Disease Rating Scale: Eli Lilly\'s combined cognition-and-function score (0 to 144, lower is worse), the primary endpoint in donanemab trials.',
    'amyloid PET': 'A brain scan using a radioactive tracer that sticks to amyloid plaques, making them visible. Expensive (thousands of dollars) and available only in larger centers.',
    'centiloid': 'A standard scale for amyloid PET results: 0 is the average of young people without amyloid, 100 is typical of mild Alzheimer\'s dementia. Below about 30 is usually read as amyloid-negative.',
    'cerebrospinal fluid': 'The clear fluid around the brain and spinal cord. Sampled with a lumbar puncture (spinal tap), it can show the amyloid and tau changes of Alzheimer\'s.',
    'ApoE4': 'A common variant of the APOE gene, the biggest genetic risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer\'s. People can carry zero, one or two copies; two copies means much higher risk of Alzheimer\'s and of ARIA on anti-amyloid drugs.',
    'homozygote': 'Someone with two identical copies of a gene variant, one from each parent. An ApoE4 homozygote carries two copies of ApoE4.',
    'heterozygote': 'Someone with two different versions of a gene: here, one ApoE4 copy and one other version.',
    'ARIA': 'Amyloid-related imaging abnormalities: swelling (ARIA-E) or small bleeds (ARIA-H) seen on MRI in people taking anti-amyloid antibodies. Usually silent; occasionally serious or fatal.',
    'ARIA-E': 'ARIA with edema: fluid leaking into brain tissue, seen as bright patches on MRI. Most cases cause no symptoms and resolve in months; some cause headache, confusion or seizures.',
    'ARIA-H': 'ARIA with hemosiderin: tiny bleeds (microhemorrhages) or iron staining on the brain surface (superficial siderosis) seen on MRI.',
    'microhemorrhage': 'A pinpoint bleed in the brain, visible on sensitive MRI sequences. Common in older people; more common in those with amyloid in their blood vessels.',
    'cerebral amyloid angiopathy': 'Amyloid deposited in the walls of the brain\'s blood vessels, making them brittle. Present in most people with Alzheimer\'s, and thought to be where ARIA begins.',
    'anticoagulant': 'A blood thinner such as apixaban (Eliquis) or warfarin that stops clots forming. Taken by millions of older people, for example for atrial fibrillation.',
    'tPA': 'Tissue plasminogen activator: a clot-dissolving drug given in the first hours of a stroke. It can cause brain bleeding, a risk that seems higher in people on anti-amyloid antibodies.',
    'MRI': 'Magnetic resonance imaging: a scanner that uses magnets rather than radiation to image soft tissue. Needed repeatedly on Leqembi to catch ARIA.',
    'Bayesian adaptive design': 'A trial design that updates the probability each option works as data arrive, and shifts new patients toward the arms that look best. Efficient, but harder to interpret and vulnerable to imbalances.',
    'open-label extension': 'A continuation after the blinded part of a trial where everyone who wants it gets the drug. Good for long-term safety data; weaker evidence on benefit.',
    'disease-modifying': 'A treatment that changes the underlying course of a disease rather than just easing its symptoms.',
    'cholinesterase inhibitor': 'An older class of Alzheimer\'s drug (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) that boosts the chemical messenger acetylcholine. Modest symptom relief; does not slow the disease.',
    'coverage with evidence development': 'A Medicare policy that pays for a treatment only if patients are enrolled in a trial or registry that collects data on how well it works.',
    'CHMP': 'Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use: the EMA committee that issues the scientific opinion on whether a drug should be approved in the EU.',
    'MHRA': 'Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency: the UK\'s drug regulator.',
    'NICE': 'National Institute for Health and Care Excellence: the body that decides whether England\'s NHS will pay for a medicine, based largely on cost-effectiveness.',
    'minimal clinically important difference': 'The smallest change in a score that patients or clinicians would notice as meaningful. Estimating it is hard and contested.',
    'subcutaneous': 'Injected just under the skin, like insulin, rather than into a vein. Can be done at home with an autoinjector.',
    'blood-brain barrier': 'The tight lining of the brain\'s blood vessels that keeps most large molecules out. Only a tiny fraction of an antibody in the blood (well under 1%) gets into the brain.',
    'IgG1': 'The most common class of antibody in the blood, and the backbone of most antibody drugs. Its stem (Fc) signals immune cells such as microglia to engulf what it binds.',
  },
